<div class="article-body prose prose-lg dark:prose-invert max-w-none">

  <p>
    Sleep changes with age. This is one of the most consistent and well-documented findings in sleep medicine — and one of the most frequently misunderstood, both by older adults themselves and by the family members who worry about them. What looks like insomnia or fragmented sleep in a 75-year-old may be a normal expression of aged sleep architecture. What looks like nothing more than "light sleeping" may be an early warning of something worth clinical attention. The difference matters enormously for how families respond, how physicians advise, and how sleep technology should be designed to serve this population.
  </p>
  <p>
    This article explores how aging reshapes sleep from the inside — the neurological, hormonal, and circadian changes that drive the shift — and how continuous, non-contact sleep monitoring with YumePad can help families and clinicians distinguish the expectable from the concerning.
  </p>

  <h2>What Aging Does to Sleep Architecture</h2>
  <p>
    The most significant age-related change in sleep is the progressive reduction in slow-wave sleep (N3, also called deep sleep or delta sleep). In young adults, N3 typically constitutes 15–20% of total sleep time. By the seventh decade, this proportion often falls to single digits — sometimes less than five percent. The physiological mechanisms behind this change involve multiple interacting factors: reduced secretion of growth hormone (which is released in bursts that coincide with N3 epochs), decreased delta wave amplitude and power as measured by electroencephalography, and changes in the homeostatic sleep drive that accumulate more slowly with age, meaning the deep-sleep pressure that builds during wakefulness is lower.
  </p>
  <p>
    REM sleep is less dramatically affected but does show changes: REM episodes occur earlier in the night, are shorter, and show less of the vivid cortical activation that characterizes young-adult REM. Total REM percentage tends to be preserved into old age, but the REM of late life is architecturally different from that of youth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Light sleep stages (N1 and N2) expand to fill the proportion that N3 has vacated. The practical consequence is that older sleepers spend more of their night in lighter stages from which they are more easily aroused. This increases the number of brief awakenings — many of which are not consciously remembered — and contributes to the subjective experience of "light sleep" or "not really sleeping."
  </p>
  <p>
    Sleep efficiency — the percentage of time in bed spent actually asleep — declines with age. A young adult with a sleep efficiency of 92–95% is typical. An older adult with 75–80% efficiency may be entirely normal for their age. This distinction is critical: applying young-adult sleep efficiency norms to an older person will produce misleading results and unnecessary anxiety.
  </p>

  <h2>The Circadian Shift: Earlier to Bed, Earlier to Rise</h2>
  <p>
    Aging produces a well-documented advance in the circadian phase — the biological clock shifts earlier, a phenomenon sometimes called advanced sleep phase disorder when extreme but more often simply "advanced sleep phase" in its normal form. Older adults naturally feel sleepy earlier in the evening and wake earlier in the morning. Attempting to fight this shift — staying up until midnight because a 10 PM bedtime "feels too early" — results in sleep that is initiated against the circadian phase, with poorer quality and more fragmentation than sleep timed with the biological clock.
  </p>
  <p>
    The circadian shift has practical implications for families living with older relatives: an 85-year-old who goes to bed at 9 PM and wakes at 5 AM has not developed a pathological condition. They are sleeping on a schedule determined by their biology. The challenge arises when the family's schedule conflicts — when the household is active and noisy during what is the elder's consolidated sleep period, or when social pressures to stay up "like everyone else" fragment their sleep.
  </p>
  <p>
    YumePad data from older adults consistently shows this earlier phase distribution: the first deep-sleep epoch occurs earlier in the night relative to bedtime, sleep efficiency is highest in the early part of the night, and the sleep period ends earlier. Recognizing this pattern as normal rather than pathological is a key function of longitudinal monitoring.
  </p>

